00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.740
So I'll just give you
a little bit more info

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:01.430
about what I'm going to cover.

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:04.042
I'm going to start with a
little bit of discussion

00:01:04.042 --> 00:01:06.250
around the state of what
Moore's Law looks like today

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.090
and how the trend's been
going the last few years, what

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.370
the implications are for
scale up applications

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:15.910
or applications that depend
on scaling vertically.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:20.650
We'll also talk about what kind
of challenges get introduced

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:23.230
and you have to start
dealing with when you start

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.370
re-architecting for more
scale out type applications

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:30.370
and tracking performance
in those types of systems.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.860
And we'll do a
real world example,

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:34.870
or talk through some
examples from real world

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:37.970
application, which I'll talk
about it when we get to it.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:39.640
And then, at the
end, we'll kind of

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.820
cover a little bit about
once we've kind of got

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:45.220
your performance
well understood,

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.380
what kinds of things you
can think about next,

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:51.220
and kind of optimize for,
and that sort of thing.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:54.580
This graph kind of
shows the Moore's law.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.610
The big question is this,
you've seen articles

00:01:57.610 --> 00:02:01.720
on Moore's Law is dead, a bunch
of information around that.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.250
Is that technically true?

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:07.210
Well, those who remember,
Moore's law is about transistor

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.650
count doubling every 18
months slash two years,

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.890
depending on who you talk to.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.350
So you can see from this
graph, transistor count

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:15.370
continues to go up.

00:02:15.370 --> 00:02:19.030
So that part of Moore's
law is not really dead,

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.850
but some of the important
characteristics that

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.510
also used to scale along
in roughly that frequency

00:02:25.510 --> 00:02:27.130
are no longer holding.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:30.700
And so, for example,
single thread performance

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:33.120
as measured by your
typical [? specing ?]

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.440
CPU type benchmark
continues to increase,

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.920
but not nearly at the
same rate as it used to.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.100
The frequency of the CPUs
themselves, as well as

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.260
the power that they can actually
draw has sort of plateaued off.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.600
There are some reasons for that.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.320
It's called the power wall.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.390
It's pretty easy--
it's basically

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:00.475
about getting that much power
to a small area of the chip,

00:03:00.475 --> 00:03:02.140
the small die area,
without basically

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:04.690
melting the whole thing.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.450
And then the number
of logical cores

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:10.360
continues to increase with
the transistor count, which

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:15.090
is good, but that kind of
signals even at the CPU

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:18.580
microarchitectural
layer they're already

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:21.340
kind of moving away from
the vertical scaling,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.590
super high frequency,
higher and higher frequency,

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:28.004
to more of a scale out
configuration architecture

00:03:28.004 --> 00:03:29.920
where they're just adding
more and more cores.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.770
And so, taking advantage
of parallelism at the CPU

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.280
level to get more
performance out

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.850
of the same amount of power.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:41.050
So how does this
impact large scale

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.300
application architectures?

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.580
Basically any of
scale up components

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.730
that you have become your
performance bottlenecks pretty

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.670
quickly, especially
if you're building

00:03:51.670 --> 00:03:53.380
a service that serves--

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:55.510
it's a mobile app or something--

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.520
10,000 users, you
can probably do that

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.340
on one SQL database
running on one VM.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:03.130
If you scale that up to
like 100 million users,

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.080
that's going to fall
down pretty quickly.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.720
So large scale
application developers

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.430
or people that are
trying to evolve

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:12.640
their architectures to support
a larger, very fast growing user

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.610
base are having
to figure out how

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.490
to re-architect to their scale
up components and systems

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:22.540
to be more hard horizontally
scalable or scale out.

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:25.420
So examples include
like charting

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:28.450
your user base or your
users across multiple shards

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:29.170
of a database.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:34.180
Maybe geo-specific-- so
all the users in Europe

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:36.220
are in one database,
all the users in the US

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.660
are in another database--
that's one example.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.420
You can do the same
thing with front-ends.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.790
You kind of build get
rid of the state that

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:46.480
sits in your front-ends
and do scale out.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:49.090
You run multiple front-ends to
terminate your inbound client

00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:51.590
connections and
things like that.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.070
But all of this also
means that as you've

00:04:54.070 --> 00:04:56.560
changed the
architecture, your system

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:58.120
becomes a lot more
complicated, it

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:00.124
gets a lot harder to
understand and reason

00:05:00.124 --> 00:05:02.290
about what the overall
system performance looks like

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:05.230
and how to troubleshoot that.

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:07.720
There's a good analogy--
if you think about

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.790
like building high
availability of systems,

00:05:10.790 --> 00:05:12.400
you've got to have redundancy.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:14.860
You want to get rid of
single points of failures.

00:05:14.860 --> 00:05:16.960
You can kind of use
a similar analogy

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:19.950
for when you're thinking about
performance of your application

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:23.230
or workload, in that if you've
got a scale up component

00:05:23.230 --> 00:05:26.050
that becomes a single point
of a performance bottleneck.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:27.550
It doesn't quite
roll off the tongue

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:29.356
like single point
of failure, but it

00:05:29.356 --> 00:05:30.730
gets the point
across and kind of

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:33.700
gives you a good mental
model to keep in mind.

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:38.440
So going for a quick example,
if this it's just a basic web

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:40.200
browser, it's just
serving some content,

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:42.370
and you've got two variations.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:45.292
On the left you've got one
where the database, and the web

00:05:45.292 --> 00:05:47.500
server, and all the tools,
and everything are sitting

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:50.110
on one machine, it's
pretty easy for me

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:53.540
to measure total QPS or
total requests per second

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:55.000
that I can process here.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:58.330
I can use my standard operating
system tools on Linux,

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:01.600
like Top and whatnot, to
see what system-- like,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.880
is the database the thing
consuming all the CPU, is it

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:06.220
my web server, or whatever.

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.830
So that's pretty easy to
understand and figure out

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:10.330
where your bottlenecks are.

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.079
And even the
example on the right

00:06:12.079 --> 00:06:13.870
where you've kind of
split that out, you've

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:15.870
now got two machines--
one that's dedicated just

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.610
for the database and one that's
then running your web server

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.104
tools.

00:06:20.104 --> 00:06:22.270
You've only got two places
to look to kind of figure

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:25.910
out what is currently
limiting your performance.

00:06:25.910 --> 00:06:30.802
So as my number of connections
grows, do I see a plateau.

00:06:30.802 --> 00:06:32.260
I need to go look
at what system is

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:36.880
the thing that's causing that
and how to architect to fix it.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:39.490
On the other hand, if I'm
building something quite a bit

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:42.340
more complicated-- like this is
an example of a real-time ads

00:06:42.340 --> 00:06:43.936
bidding application--

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.910
if my bids per second
are hitting a cap,

00:06:51.910 --> 00:06:55.210
and/or the latency
of each bit is

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:00.580
increasing as my number of bids
that I try to submit goes up,

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:04.570
how do I know where in
here the bottleneck is?

00:07:04.570 --> 00:07:08.140
So maybe it's at the load
balancer, probably not.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.930
It could be I'm not adding
enough Compute servers.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:13.690
The bidding servers-- I might
not be scaling and adding

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:15.020
enough of them.

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:17.840
It could be many of the
different components here.

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:20.050
And so, understanding
which one it is it

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:22.780
becomes a lot more difficult.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.150
And then, once you kind of--

00:07:25.150 --> 00:07:27.650
not just performance
of your application,

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:33.160
but also, am I spending too
much money to deliver this?

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:35.200
In a system like this,
what's the most likely?

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:36.970
This is genomics
analysis, so you've

00:07:36.970 --> 00:07:39.040
got a bunch of gene
sequencers that

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.830
are doing a gene
sequencing analysis,

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:46.930
dumping all that data into VMs
to ingest into this big HPC

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.780
cluster that also runs on VMs.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:52.420
Of all these systems, which
one is probably the most likely

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:55.840
I'm overpaying for, or
that I can optimize or tune

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:57.640
to be more efficient,
or maybe try

00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:00.340
a different type of
hardware component,

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.640
like optimize my computation
for GPUs for example.

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:05.487
And if I can get more
throughput that way,

00:08:05.487 --> 00:08:07.570
that it might end up being
cheaper than continuing

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.220
to add more CPU VMs.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:12.040
So that's just another example.

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.064
So earlier, I said we were going
to talk through some examples

00:08:15.064 --> 00:08:17.230
from the real world, because
I think examples really

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.860
help cement these ideas.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:23.710
And I'm going to use
examples from Compute Engine

00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:26.020
itself, which is the
service that I work on.

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:27.940
And it's an infrastructure
as a service.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:31.340
We rent VMs by the second.

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:36.704
And it's basically, A, a very
complicated, complex service,

00:08:36.704 --> 00:08:38.620
it's got lot of different
components-- control

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:41.885
plane, data plane, actual VMs,
the underlying host machines,

00:08:41.885 --> 00:08:44.650
the KVM host kernel
environment, and lots

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:47.140
of different components
to think about.

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:50.900
It's also got lots of customers
and is growing pretty rapidly,

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.370
so we're having to
re-architect different pieces

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:56.020
as the service
continues to grow,

00:08:56.020 --> 00:08:59.760
and kind of identifying
which pieces those are

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:02.030
is kind of part of
this whole process.

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:04.570
And then, there's
obviously a huge variety

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:05.890
of use cases in infrastructure.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:08.980
So people can use it for
whatever they currently have,

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.940
want to run, want to build.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:12.670
So there is a lot of
different use cases,

00:09:12.670 --> 00:09:14.920
a lot of different requirements,
different performance

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:17.590
characteristics that
different applications need,

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.300
and et cetera.

00:09:19.300 --> 00:09:22.000
And this is kind of
the journey that we've

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.130
been on since we
launched the service

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.660
many years ago at this point.

00:09:26.660 --> 00:09:29.680
And so, providing
examples from this I think

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:32.020
will help cement it.

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:36.250
So kind of step one for any
kind of performance analysis

00:09:36.250 --> 00:09:38.200
that you want to do
on a complex system

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:41.170
is measure everything
you can think of.

00:09:41.170 --> 00:09:43.450
We think in terms of what
are called service level

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:44.110
indicators.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:46.750
And, essentially, a service
level indicator is simply

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:50.590
a metric that you want to
track, like number of VM exits,

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:55.150
for example, or plus the reason
for the VM exit, cache misses,

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:57.260
TLB misses, that sort of thing.

00:09:57.260 --> 00:09:59.500
Those are all SLIs
that you can figure out

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:03.010
how to measure, and then
start analyzing over time,

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.350
and build dashboards around,
and start correlating

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.060
with potentially
performance issues

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.100
that your customers have.

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.440
Those are examples
at the VM level.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.870
Higher up in the control
plane stack, like latency

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:14.830
of APIs requests.

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:18.790
Like, every time an API request
comes in for creating a VM,

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:21.400
how long does that take
before I can respond back?

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:23.460
And how long does it take
for the VM to spin up?

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:24.418
And that sort of thing.

00:10:24.418 --> 00:10:27.899
So these are all examples of
the service level indicators

00:10:27.899 --> 00:10:28.690
that are important.

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:32.920
And a good mental
model is, ask yourself,

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:36.070
what performance related things
do my customers care about?

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:37.970
Do they care about
spin up time of a VM?

00:10:37.970 --> 00:10:38.830
Probably.

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:42.760
Do they care about the
number of TLB exits?

00:10:42.760 --> 00:10:45.275
They probably don't know how
that affects their performance.

00:10:45.275 --> 00:10:46.900
But if they have a
performance problem,

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:49.310
we could use that to
help troubleshoot it.

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:52.190
So that's another good example.

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:55.090
And just to kind of give you
some numbers, some examples,

00:10:55.090 --> 00:10:57.620
you're probably not going
to start out quite this big.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.070
And we didn't start out
this big either, but just

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:03.070
the jobs that run the virtual
machines themselves export

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:07.600
over 6,000 of these SLIs,
basically, every few seconds.

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:09.910
And so, we have a
massive number of these

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:11.980
that we analyze and collect.

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:16.090
And 2,200 of that 6,000 just
come from the virtual machine

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:18.850
monitor itself, the process
that is sitting there

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:22.800
kind of monitoring the VM and
what it's doing, and helping

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.400
do the I/O operations
and things like that.

00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:27.620
So you know you
won't start that big,

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:29.740
but it's pretty easy to
get that big if you're

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:33.320
building a complicated system.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:35.970
So here's an example I referred
to a little bit earlier,

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:37.270
like creating an instance.

00:11:37.270 --> 00:11:40.600
This is probably one of the
most important operations

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.900
that a service that
sells VMs is doing.

00:11:43.900 --> 00:11:47.260
So customers tend to
care a lot about it.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:49.240
And when we measure
this, this graph

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.760
is actually from an
internal dashboard

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:54.160
that we have that measures
this kind of constantly.

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:56.500
So the vertical
axis is how long it

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:59.230
takes to create
the VM, basically

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:01.540
from the time the Compute
Engine receives the API

00:12:01.540 --> 00:12:04.720
requests to when the guest OS
finishes booting, because we

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:07.116
have a signal for that.

00:12:07.116 --> 00:12:08.740
It hits the metadata
server, and that's

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:11.590
how we tell when it
finishes booting.

00:12:11.590 --> 00:12:15.140
And then the horizontal axis
is just kind of over time.

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:17.380
So we've been measuring
this for many years.

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:22.300
So I just took a snapshot
of this dashboard

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:23.320
for a few months.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:27.820
I think it's over like a six
month period a while back.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:31.690
So once I've got a lot of
metrics that I'm gathering,

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:33.380
what do I do with them?

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:38.140
So the next step is to
set targets for what

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:40.330
those metrics should be.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:43.720
And again, we started with
service level indicators,

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.220
that's the raw
metrics themselves.

00:12:45.220 --> 00:12:47.500
Now, we talk about
service level objectives.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:51.310
And a service level objective
is basically like, all right,

00:12:51.310 --> 00:12:55.900
well, here's what I'm measuring
for, let's say, VM create time,

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.290
and it's on the order
of 35, 40 seconds.

00:12:59.290 --> 00:13:00.550
That sounds good.

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:02.470
Is that good enough?

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:04.835
Do I want to set my SLO
at where I am today?

00:13:04.835 --> 00:13:06.460
Am I getting customers
that are already

00:13:06.460 --> 00:13:09.250
complaining that my VM
take too long to spin up?

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:12.850
Maybe that is a good
signal that tells me, well,

00:13:12.850 --> 00:13:15.690
where I am today is not
maybe where I need to be.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:18.400
So setting the SLO
more aggressively

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:20.560
and then figuring out what
I need to do to hit that

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:23.890
is definitely a good
way to go about it.

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:26.797
Many of you, if you've
read the SRE handbook,

00:13:26.797 --> 00:13:29.380
you'll probably recognize some
of these terms that I'm using--

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:31.810
SLOs, SLIs.

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:35.950
These are all metrics that our
SRE teams use for availability

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.150
of a service as well.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:41.157
And they map very well to
performance availability,

00:13:41.157 --> 00:13:42.490
if you can think of it that way.

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:44.420
Is my performance consistent?

00:13:44.420 --> 00:13:47.980
And what consistent
level am I delivering it?

00:13:47.980 --> 00:13:50.410
What happens when
I fall out of that?

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:51.430
Et cetera.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:55.360
So you set the objective, your
customers can help tell you

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:57.340
when or how to set
that objective,

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.480
or what to set it to.

00:13:59.480 --> 00:14:01.780
And if there's no
obvious target and you

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:04.509
haven't gotten any complaints
about a particular performance

00:14:04.509 --> 00:14:06.550
characteristic, it's
probably fine to set the SLO

00:14:06.550 --> 00:14:10.240
where it is today, and then
when you start getting feedback

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.240
from customers that it
might not be good enough,

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:16.270
then reconsider where
you would set it.

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:19.150
Another key thing
about SLOs is you

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:21.629
have to have kind of similar--

00:14:21.629 --> 00:14:23.920
there's a lot of details in
the SRE handbook about this

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:27.250
as well, but you have
to have a plan of action

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:31.570
for either, if I set my
SLO at a better target

00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:34.030
than where I can deliver
today, you need to have a plan

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:36.760
to get it to that new target.

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.940
You also need a
reaction plan if you

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:44.470
set it to roughly where you're
delivering performance today,

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:47.170
the reaction plan is, well, what
happens if all of a sudden I

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:48.640
stopped meeting that SLO?

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:50.590
Let's say I did a
push of a new binary

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:53.740
or a new component of my system,
and all of a sudden my VM

00:14:53.740 --> 00:14:57.054
create time went below SLO.

00:14:57.054 --> 00:14:58.720
I have a certain
amount of time to react

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:01.060
to that, that's my SLO budget.

00:15:01.060 --> 00:15:05.530
And the reaction plan really
needs to be very quantified.

00:15:05.530 --> 00:15:10.790
For something like VM create, if
we go from creating VMs in 30,

00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:12.760
40 seconds to
taking five minutes,

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:15.080
that could break many
customers' applications.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:19.570
And so it's critical that the
plan be immediately actionable.

00:15:19.570 --> 00:15:21.354
If it was a roll out,
then roll it back.

00:15:21.354 --> 00:15:23.270
Get back to the state
where we're meeting SLO,

00:15:23.270 --> 00:15:26.560
then go figure out what
went wrong, go fix the bug

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:29.850
that in that push,
whatever the case may be.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:34.000
So that's essentially,
the purpose of the SLOs,

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.670
and the importance of
having that scripted action

00:15:36.670 --> 00:15:37.625
plan ahead of time.

00:15:37.625 --> 00:15:40.000
Because if you don't have
that, and the first time you go

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.490
[? out ?] [? of ?] SLO, you're
scrambling to try to figure out

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:43.320
what you should do.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:46.930
There should be a really
established playbook for that.

00:15:46.930 --> 00:15:50.290
So just to kind of give
you a visual example

00:15:50.290 --> 00:15:53.890
of creating the SLO, we go back
to the create-instance example.

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:57.070
And essentially,
setting the SLO is

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:59.160
like drawing a line
at where I think

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.340
is acceptable for
create performance.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:04.540
And obviously, since the
vertical axis is time,

00:16:04.540 --> 00:16:05.990
lower is better for VM create.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:07.740
That means VMs are
getting created faster.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:12.190
So the other lines are actually
some of our internal systems

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:15.520
that we measured, like different
components and different flows

00:16:15.520 --> 00:16:19.460
of our internal provisioning
and resource allocation systems.

00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:22.840
And so that's why this graph has
a lot of different bars on it.

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:26.640
But that top bar is the one
that measures when the guest

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.560
OS is finished booting.

00:16:29.560 --> 00:16:31.870
So the next step of
this whole process

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:34.000
is basically go back to step 1.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:37.810
You are going to do this over
and over and over, especially

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:41.320
as you're iterating
on your application,

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:42.910
your user base is
growing, you're

00:16:42.910 --> 00:16:45.760
finding these new
architectural limitations

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:49.480
or scale up components that
are currently your bottleneck,

00:16:49.480 --> 00:16:53.030
you're reevaluating
where you have set SLOs,

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:55.030
because a customer
you know reported

00:16:55.030 --> 00:16:57.550
that they had an issue
at such and such a time.

00:16:57.550 --> 00:17:00.250
You go back and look at what
your telemetry says, and of,

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:02.470
but we were within SLO.

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:04.510
Maybe that means we
need to change the SLO

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:07.310
and start figuring
out how to do better.

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:08.980
So if you get enough
customers that

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:10.900
are complaining about
a specific thing

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:12.704
not working well
enough or fast enough,

00:17:12.704 --> 00:17:14.079
that's usually a
really good sign

00:17:14.079 --> 00:17:18.099
that you have to readjust
and rethink that SLO.

00:17:18.099 --> 00:17:20.470
And basically, you
have customers.

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:21.940
As your customer
base grows, you're

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:23.770
going to be doing
investigations with them,

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:25.270
they're going to
have issues, you're

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:27.130
going to go look
at your telemetry,

00:17:27.130 --> 00:17:29.440
you're not going
to find anything

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:31.192
that is measuring
exactly what it

00:17:31.192 --> 00:17:32.650
is that's causing
them the problem,

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:34.870
and so you're going to
work with them on a repro,

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:37.420
you're going to have them
repro it, whatever the case,

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:40.300
you'll eventually start
adding additional SLIs,

00:17:40.300 --> 00:17:41.770
defining new SLOs, et cetera.

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:43.870
So this is a very
iterative process,

00:17:43.870 --> 00:17:48.220
and will continue as
new investigations go

00:17:48.220 --> 00:17:51.370
on, new troubleshoot with
customers, you'll add new SLIs

00:17:51.370 --> 00:17:56.560
and set new SLOs, at some point
that kind of process stops,

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:00.100
and you are no longer
finding new things to add,

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:03.040
but you're still encountering
customers having issues,

00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:06.370
and basically your internal
telemetry has taken you

00:18:06.370 --> 00:18:09.430
as far as it can when it comes
to measuring what the customer

00:18:09.430 --> 00:18:10.630
performances is.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.300
And, at that point
you have to become

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:14.770
a customer of your own service.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:20.420
So essentially,
you want to build

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:24.310
you're SLIs in a way
that it directly reflects

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:25.480
what the customer sees.

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:31.030
And a good example of that is,
my original metric was, time

00:18:31.030 --> 00:18:33.310
to when the guest
OS finished booting.

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:34.720
That's not the whole story.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.980
Most customer applications,
most users, if I'm SSH,

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:40.480
like you they don't care when
the guest OS finishes booting,

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:42.370
they care when they can
actually get access to it

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:43.400
or it can start serving.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:46.700
So it probably needs network
to be provisioned as well.

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:52.030
So basically a better
customer facing metric

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:54.460
is from the time
we received the API

00:18:54.460 --> 00:18:57.550
call to when you can SSH to it.

00:18:57.550 --> 00:18:59.677
And those new lines
that you see--

00:18:59.677 --> 00:19:02.260
that orange line in the middle,
that was the original guest OS

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:05.650
boot line, and I've just added
like the network provisioning

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:07.415
steps and network
path programming--

00:19:07.415 --> 00:19:09.790
internal and external network
programming, and all that--

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:11.050
to this graph.

00:19:11.050 --> 00:19:15.370
And comically enough-- it
wasn't funny at the time--

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:17.950
but this was when we learned
that our network provisioning

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:21.280
time scaled roughly with how
big our service was getting,

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.350
and figured out that
we have to go fix that

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:25.690
and re-architect network
programming model

00:19:25.690 --> 00:19:27.440
to make sure that
doesn't happen.

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:30.430
So again, you get really
interesting signals

00:19:30.430 --> 00:19:32.680
when you start measuring
from the external customers

00:19:32.680 --> 00:19:35.260
perspective, rather than just
relying on your internal SLIs.

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:42.070
So what we end up
having to do, is we

00:19:42.070 --> 00:19:44.172
built a service that
measures performance

00:19:44.172 --> 00:19:45.130
just like the customer.

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:46.420
So it runs benchmarks.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:48.010
It can run workloads.

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:50.110
It's called perfkit benchmarker.

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:50.955
It's open source.

00:19:50.955 --> 00:19:51.580
It's on GitHub.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:54.010
You just search GitHub
for perfkit benchmarker,

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:54.720
you'll find it.

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:57.850
There's also perfkit
explorer, which is basically

00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:00.700
a dashboarding and analytics
framework that makes it easier

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:02.290
to build graphs,
kind of like the ones

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:05.490
that you saw in
some of my examples.

00:20:05.490 --> 00:20:11.320
And you start using PKB
or a PKB-like service--

00:20:11.320 --> 00:20:12.990
so it depends on
whatever your services,

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:14.862
but you basically end
up building something

00:20:14.862 --> 00:20:17.070
that measures the performance
like your customers do.

00:20:17.070 --> 00:20:20.160
So if it's a mobile
application, you

00:20:20.160 --> 00:20:23.760
are probably gathering
telemetry out at the client end.

00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:28.014
You're running tests that mimic
client behavior connecting

00:20:28.014 --> 00:20:28.680
to your servers.

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:30.330
Maybe you do it on
actual mobile devices,

00:20:30.330 --> 00:20:32.330
maybe you do it on
emulators, whatever the case.

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:34.230
But having an
automated framework

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:36.660
that lets you launch these
tests and rerun them constantly

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:38.310
in all of your
environments, including

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:39.690
your pre-production
environments,

00:20:39.690 --> 00:20:41.606
like your developer
environments, your staging

00:20:41.606 --> 00:20:45.570
environments, those become
critical to catching issues,

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:47.910
regressions, SLO
violations, et cetera

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:52.230
as you develop and build
new versions of your product

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:53.340
or application.

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:58.980
So the key with measuring
your own service,

00:20:58.980 --> 00:21:00.180
it's a prober-based system.

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:03.550
So you're probing from the
perspective of the customer.

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:06.240
So in the VM, like in
the example before,

00:21:06.240 --> 00:21:11.670
we were probing by basically
running a service that measures

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:14.070
when I can SSH to the VM.

00:21:14.070 --> 00:21:15.930
So that means I run
something inside the VM

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:18.794
that lets me know
when that succeeds.

00:21:18.794 --> 00:21:21.210
And a lot of the prober-based
benchmarks are the same way.

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:24.229
You spin up a VM, you
install whatever benchmark

00:21:24.229 --> 00:21:25.770
you want to run,
you run it, and then

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.770
you collect the results
back, and graph the results

00:21:28.770 --> 00:21:30.930
and see how things are going.

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:33.760
So now we'll do a quick demo.

00:21:33.760 --> 00:21:35.010
Can you flip over to the demo?

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:43.270
So the demo I'm going to
run is TCP round robin,

00:21:43.270 --> 00:21:46.360
which is basically
part of the PKB suite,

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:47.800
but it's a netperf
benchmark that

00:21:47.800 --> 00:21:51.010
measures the VM to VM latency--

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:53.860
round trip latency between
two virtual machines.

00:21:53.860 --> 00:21:56.590
So I'm just going to
kick off the demo first,

00:21:56.590 --> 00:21:58.840
and then I'll talk a little
bit about what it's doing.

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:00.340
It takes a few
minutes, so I'm going

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:04.580
to have to dance around
while it's actually running.

00:22:04.580 --> 00:22:09.430
So let's quickly take a look
at the actual script itself.

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:11.440
All the script is
doing is basically

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:18.880
calling PKB, which is the main
kind of orchestration script,

00:22:18.880 --> 00:22:21.180
and gives it a
bunch of arguments.

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:23.710
One of the arguments
is netperfconfig.yaml,

00:22:23.710 --> 00:22:26.450
so I'll pull that
up in a minute.

00:22:26.450 --> 00:22:30.730
But basically, once
PKB finishes its runs,

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:34.000
it collects all the data
in a local directory,

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:38.710
runs a plotting tool
that creates a nice CDF,

00:22:38.710 --> 00:22:41.710
and then uploads the CDF to
a storage bucket, which I'll

00:22:41.710 --> 00:22:43.600
pull up as soon as it finishes.

00:22:43.600 --> 00:22:47.410
So the CDF is basically
just a distribution

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:49.746
a graph that shows the
distribution of what

00:22:49.746 --> 00:22:50.870
the performance looks like.

00:22:50.870 --> 00:22:52.640
So as a cumulative
distribution function,

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:54.550
it's a really nice
way to look at what

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:57.610
my average performance is,
what long-tail performance is.

00:22:57.610 --> 00:23:00.650
It kind of shows you that graph,
and I'll show you in a minute,

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:02.780
it'll make more sense.

00:23:02.780 --> 00:23:07.610
A couple of notes about
the benchmarks themselves

00:23:07.610 --> 00:23:09.910
and kind of the
guidelines around this--

00:23:09.910 --> 00:23:13.120
actually, let me quickly
pull up the config file.

00:23:18.790 --> 00:23:21.010
So it's pretty
straightforward as well.

00:23:21.010 --> 00:23:24.400
All it's saying is I'm
using N1 standard 2 VMs.

00:23:24.400 --> 00:23:27.630
I'm using the internal
network to do my measurement.

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:29.760
I'm not going out
through external IPs.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:31.410
The benchmark I want to run.

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:35.880
How many histogram buckets
I want to collect for it.

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:38.160
And I actually ran the
multi-zone argument,

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:40.980
which means it's spinning
up 10 pairs of VMs

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:45.090
in three different zones
across three different regions

00:23:45.090 --> 00:23:50.460
and running the RR benchmark for
60 seconds on each pair of VMs

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:54.990
to measure to get a good
sample size of the round trip

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:59.110
time between VMs in those zones.

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:02.031
So let's see how it's doing.

00:24:02.031 --> 00:24:02.530
Cool.

00:24:02.530 --> 00:24:04.390
Yeah, it's still running.

00:24:04.390 --> 00:24:07.870
You can see it's SSHing in
to the VMs running the tool.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:10.210
And it will be
pulling the data back.

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:12.850
So kind of talking
a little bit more

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:15.034
generally about benchmarking--

00:24:15.034 --> 00:24:17.200
how to pick the how to pick
your benchmarks that you

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:18.970
want to use.

00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:21.555
Microbenchmarks are
usually a good start.

00:24:21.555 --> 00:24:25.040
A microbenchmark like
SPECint, SPEC CPU, Coremark.

00:24:25.040 --> 00:24:28.280
TCP-RR is actually also
considered a microbenchmark,

00:24:28.280 --> 00:24:31.180
but it's a little bit
more representative

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.940
of what an actual real
application might want to do,

00:24:33.940 --> 00:24:35.560
or a performance
characteristic that

00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:40.360
might be valuable to understand
for a real application.

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:41.867
Microbenchmarks in
and of themselves

00:24:41.867 --> 00:24:44.200
are not always a good indicator
of what your workload is

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:46.782
going to experience.

00:24:46.782 --> 00:24:49.240
Real workloads tend to be a
lot more complicated than that.

00:24:49.240 --> 00:24:51.910
And so, you can start
with microbenchmarks,

00:24:51.910 --> 00:24:54.260
but you should
probably have a plan

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:56.650
for doing something
that's more like running

00:24:56.650 --> 00:24:59.340
things that are more like what
your customers are running.

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:03.010
For GCE, that includes running
benchmarks and microbenchmarks

00:25:03.010 --> 00:25:03.820
in combination.

00:25:03.820 --> 00:25:06.280
So maybe I'll fire up you
know SPECint or Coremark,

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:07.150
or whatever.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:09.850
And at the same time, I'm
also spinning up a netperf

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:11.290
to do some network traffic.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:14.174
TCPstream maybe to
push a bunch of network

00:25:14.174 --> 00:25:16.340
either out to the internet
or some internet endpoint

00:25:16.340 --> 00:25:17.340
or between VMs.

00:25:17.340 --> 00:25:20.110
I might also spin up
like an IOstat or FIO

00:25:20.110 --> 00:25:21.490
benchmark at the
same time, which

00:25:21.490 --> 00:25:24.520
is hammering on my storage,
whether it's local SSD, or PD,

00:25:24.520 --> 00:25:25.610
or what have you.

00:25:25.610 --> 00:25:29.200
So now I've got the VM actually
doing a combination of things,

00:25:29.200 --> 00:25:31.000
and I can start really
getting a sense for,

00:25:31.000 --> 00:25:34.480
oh, well, when I start
doing a lot of disk IO,

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:36.315
my CPU benchmark slows
down for some reason.

00:25:36.315 --> 00:25:37.690
You should probably
go understand

00:25:37.690 --> 00:25:40.450
why that is, because chances
are customers are probably

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:43.420
going to be doing things more
complicated than just running

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:49.090
one CPU-heavy benchmark,
or CPU-heavy workload.

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:51.250
And then, better yet
then combinations

00:25:51.250 --> 00:25:53.810
of microbenchmarks, is actually
workload-based benchmarks.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:58.630
So you can get a sense-- for
GCE, we know lots of customers

00:25:58.630 --> 00:26:01.780
run NoSQL databases
and Apache LAMP

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:03.820
stacks for web serving
and stuff like that.

00:26:03.820 --> 00:26:05.950
So there are open-source
benchmarks out there

00:26:05.950 --> 00:26:09.040
that will measure the
throughput of a Cassandra

00:26:09.040 --> 00:26:12.380
cluster or a MongoDB cluster.

00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:15.160
Aerospike's got a
great set of test tools

00:26:15.160 --> 00:26:18.880
for their in-memory
NoSQL database.

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:21.779
So find the benchmarks
or find the workloads

00:26:21.779 --> 00:26:23.320
that your customers
are running, even

00:26:23.320 --> 00:26:28.450
if it's just a piece of it,
and start measuring those.

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:32.230
And then, kind of
the better still

00:26:32.230 --> 00:26:35.470
is when you actually can run
exactly what your customers are

00:26:35.470 --> 00:26:37.300
running in exactly
the same config.

00:26:37.300 --> 00:26:39.670
And that's not always possible.

00:26:39.670 --> 00:26:43.060
For us, we sometimes
can, we sometimes can't.

00:26:43.060 --> 00:26:45.060
We can get a sense for
what they might be doing,

00:26:45.060 --> 00:26:47.643
we'll have a meeting with them,
talk about what they're doing,

00:26:47.643 --> 00:26:49.780
and then try to
replicate that experience

00:26:49.780 --> 00:26:53.290
in our own environment
using similar configs.

00:26:53.290 --> 00:26:56.140
Sometimes we actually can work
with them and get the benchmark

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:58.230
or get the workload that
they're actually running,

00:26:58.230 --> 00:26:59.400
get a copy of it.

00:26:59.400 --> 00:27:01.390
And they just give us
a bunch of dummy data,

00:27:01.390 --> 00:27:03.070
and we can do the
same exact thing

00:27:03.070 --> 00:27:05.140
that they're doing,
just with data

00:27:05.140 --> 00:27:08.674
that is kind of placeholder.

00:27:08.674 --> 00:27:09.340
All right, cool.

00:27:09.340 --> 00:27:10.070
So it finished.

00:27:10.070 --> 00:27:11.730
It took about a few minutes.

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:14.390
So I managed to cover the time.

00:27:14.390 --> 00:27:17.440
So I'm just going to go to the
GCS bucket now, refresh it,

00:27:17.440 --> 00:27:21.790
and you can see the timestamp
on it is just a few seconds ago.

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:23.650
And so, it did--
it actually worked.

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:28.559
Woohoo, demo gods
smiled upon me.

00:27:28.559 --> 00:27:30.350
So this is what I was
talking about of CDF.

00:27:30.350 --> 00:27:34.520
So this shows you what the
distribution of your VM

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:37.000
to VM latency is in
three different zones

00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:39.370
that we have that are across
three different regions.

00:27:39.370 --> 00:27:43.370
And so, kind of the
50th percentile.

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:46.030
This is like your average,
median, round trip

00:27:46.030 --> 00:27:47.470
time between VMs.

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:49.240
And then you can see,
up toward the top,

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:52.600
some zones are slightly better
in the long tail than others.

00:27:52.600 --> 00:27:56.170
But the more vertical
this is, the better.

00:27:56.170 --> 00:27:58.480
So this is pretty good
indicator that things

00:27:58.480 --> 00:28:02.630
are working reasonably
well here in these zones.

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:04.000
OK, we can flip back to the--

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:07.946
cool.

00:28:07.946 --> 00:28:12.470
Let's see-- OK, so now
I've got my SLOs defined.

00:28:12.470 --> 00:28:13.540
I'm meeting them all.

00:28:13.540 --> 00:28:17.880
I can catch regressions in
new versions of my system

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:20.570
before they hit production.

00:28:20.570 --> 00:28:21.590
What now?

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:23.880
What's kind of the
next step from here?

00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.380
A lot of that then
becomes turning your focus

00:28:27.380 --> 00:28:29.960
toward making your
systems more efficient.

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:32.780
You've probably got a backlog
of some re-architecting

00:28:32.780 --> 00:28:35.090
you need to do, depending
on how fast your customer

00:28:35.090 --> 00:28:39.740
base is growing, or how
your user base is growing.

00:28:39.740 --> 00:28:45.160
So am I spending way too much
money on solving this problem?

00:28:45.160 --> 00:28:47.840
Is there something I could
be doing more efficient?

00:28:47.840 --> 00:28:49.370
Is there some product
that I can use

00:28:49.370 --> 00:28:52.100
that gives me a better TCO--

00:28:52.100 --> 00:28:55.040
Total Cost Per whatever
it is I'm trying to do?

00:28:55.040 --> 00:28:57.470
And kind of to that
point, it really helps

00:28:57.470 --> 00:29:01.060
to start thinking about, what
is your cost for performance?

00:29:01.060 --> 00:29:05.420
So what is it costing
me to hit, for example,

00:29:05.420 --> 00:29:08.060
the 30-second
create time for VMs?

00:29:08.060 --> 00:29:10.740
How much more would
it cost me engineering

00:29:10.740 --> 00:29:12.740
investment-wise, resource-wise?

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:15.630
Maybe I can shard my
control plane even further

00:29:15.630 --> 00:29:17.780
to let it scale up further.

00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:20.840
What's that going to cost me
to get another 10 seconds off

00:29:20.840 --> 00:29:22.160
of my VM create time?

00:29:22.160 --> 00:29:25.714
So thinking about the
performance, and cost

00:29:25.714 --> 00:29:27.380
of the performance
you're trying to get,

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:29.990
and how much it
would take to get

00:29:29.990 --> 00:29:34.700
kind of next level performance
really helps with this process.

00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:37.280
Another example is, if I'm
doing ML image training--

00:29:37.280 --> 00:29:39.590
I'm training and
machine learning model

00:29:39.590 --> 00:29:41.780
to do image recognition.

00:29:41.780 --> 00:29:46.070
During the training, what's
my pennies per image, right?

00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:47.900
Hopefully, it's not
even pennies per image.

00:29:47.900 --> 00:29:50.390
If it is, you're
probably doing it wrong.

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:52.280
And then, once you've
got it trained,

00:29:52.280 --> 00:29:54.890
what's my classification cost?

00:29:54.890 --> 00:29:58.160
How many pennies per
classification am I spending?

00:29:58.160 --> 00:30:01.560
And can I do better than that?

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:05.060
So find the products
that fit your patterns.

00:30:05.060 --> 00:30:06.350
What you want to do--

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:09.410
these are some
examples for GCE, but I

00:30:09.410 --> 00:30:12.200
think this is fairly
generally applicable.

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:16.020
You want to find discounts
where you can get it.

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:18.650
So for our committed
use discounts are really

00:30:18.650 --> 00:30:20.360
good for any time
you've got like kind

00:30:20.360 --> 00:30:24.740
of a steady amount of workload,
steady some number of VMs,

00:30:24.740 --> 00:30:28.442
if you buy committed use
discount that kind of covers

00:30:28.442 --> 00:30:29.900
the number of
resources that you're

00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:32.120
using for where
the green line is,

00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:35.720
you're getting a really, really
good discount, up to like 57%,

00:30:35.720 --> 00:30:38.390
I think, discount if
you buy a three-year CUD

00:30:38.390 --> 00:30:43.130
for the cost of that steady
state use amount of resources.

00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:45.080
So it works really well.

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:47.480
If you have diurnal
workloads, that

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:49.640
changes the equation
a little bit.

00:30:49.640 --> 00:30:51.790
But for the steady
state stuff, CUDs

00:30:51.790 --> 00:30:54.770
is a really great way to
save a bunch of money.

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:55.700
Sorry, too quick.

00:30:55.700 --> 00:30:57.170
Wrong button.

00:30:57.170 --> 00:31:00.290
Preemptable VMs-- if you've just
got batch-style computation,

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:03.624
that you need to launch a bunch
of VMs, do some computation,

00:31:03.624 --> 00:31:05.540
and then just delete
them all, preemptable VMs

00:31:05.540 --> 00:31:07.380
is a great model for that.

00:31:07.380 --> 00:31:09.860
They're 80% cheaper
than normal VMS,

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:13.670
and as long as you've
got a worker based system

00:31:13.670 --> 00:31:16.035
that can handle a worker
going away, remembers

00:31:16.035 --> 00:31:18.410
what work it gave to the one
that just died, and gives it

00:31:18.410 --> 00:31:21.110
to another one, this is a really
good way to save a lot of money

00:31:21.110 --> 00:31:22.340
as well.

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:24.290
Custom machine types--
it's another feature

00:31:24.290 --> 00:31:26.300
that we have for GCE
that basically makes it

00:31:26.300 --> 00:31:31.290
so that you can create your
VM to fit your workload,

00:31:31.290 --> 00:31:34.460
not try to figure out how to
get your workload to scale up

00:31:34.460 --> 00:31:37.970
to use the rest of the resources
that you have on a fixed shape

00:31:37.970 --> 00:31:40.340
VM.

00:31:40.340 --> 00:31:44.900
Other examples--
the ala cart nature

00:31:44.900 --> 00:31:47.450
of being able to add
things like local SSD,

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:52.160
different GPUs, you can add that
to any machine type or any VM,

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:54.560
as opposed to only being able
to pick for a subset of VM.

00:31:54.560 --> 00:31:58.160
So that's also another
way to help save money.

00:31:58.160 --> 00:32:02.330
If it turns out that being
able to run your workload,

00:32:02.330 --> 00:32:03.890
and maybe you find
a library that's

00:32:03.890 --> 00:32:07.010
optimized to do a specific
computation against the GPU,

00:32:07.010 --> 00:32:10.100
and it's significantly
cheaper than doing it on CPU--

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:12.860
like, being able to
add a GPU to the VM

00:32:12.860 --> 00:32:16.190
is pretty helpful without
having to spend a bunch of money

00:32:16.190 --> 00:32:18.920
also on local SSD,
perhaps, or whatever

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:19.880
else might be attached.

00:32:22.400 --> 00:32:26.769
So just to kind of recap,
start by just measuring

00:32:26.769 --> 00:32:27.935
everything you can think of.

00:32:27.935 --> 00:32:31.000
Think about what's important to
your customers, focus on that.

00:32:31.000 --> 00:32:33.740
Define the SLOs
based on either where

00:32:33.740 --> 00:32:35.690
your customers are complaining.

00:32:35.690 --> 00:32:38.090
Or if they're not complaining,
probably setting them

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:40.794
to where it is today is fine.

00:32:40.794 --> 00:32:42.710
You're going to go through
that process a lot.

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:44.490
You're going to get
customers complaining.

00:32:44.490 --> 00:32:45.380
You're going to investigate.

00:32:45.380 --> 00:32:47.546
You're going to find new
things you need to measure,

00:32:47.546 --> 00:32:50.270
new SLOs you need to
set, existing SLOs

00:32:50.270 --> 00:32:52.070
need to set higher
or lower depending

00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:53.960
on what you're measuring.

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:56.480
And then, eventually,
you'll have

00:32:56.480 --> 00:32:58.322
to start measuring
as your customers do,

00:32:58.322 --> 00:32:59.780
or from your
customers perspective,

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:01.894
and building a service--

00:33:01.894 --> 00:33:03.560
or it might not be a
service, but that's

00:33:03.560 --> 00:33:07.020
the easy conceptual
way to think about it,

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:10.020
build a service for measuring
the performance of your system

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:13.260
as if it's from what the
customer's experience.

00:33:13.260 --> 00:33:16.110
So I just wanted
to give a shout out

00:33:16.110 --> 00:33:19.090
to some folks that helped
me with this, Al Hamel.

00:33:19.090 --> 00:33:21.900
He runs the performance
engineering team.

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:23.550
They built PKB.

00:33:23.550 --> 00:33:27.090
They built the infrastructure
to run all that stuff internally

00:33:27.090 --> 00:33:28.560
for GCE.

00:33:28.560 --> 00:33:31.890
He's very intimately involved
with a lot of our customers,

00:33:31.890 --> 00:33:33.900
working on analyzing
their performance

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:34.920
and stuff like that.

00:33:34.920 --> 00:33:36.510
Eric Hankland--
actually, thank him

00:33:36.510 --> 00:33:40.090
for my demo succeeding, because
of firewalls and whatnot.

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:42.720
And then Bryan was one
of our marketing people

00:33:42.720 --> 00:33:46.290
that helped me rehearse,
basically-- counting my umms.

